Oh, it does work in debug mode.

There seems to be some controversy as to it working in a scoring game,
however. Two people have already sent me mail pointing out a possible
flaw, ie. all wishes in a scoring game for the Amulet of Yendor will be
"cheap plastic imitations". No one can verified this yet however, and
unfortunately briefly looking at the source has been inconclusive so far.

Also someone has stated that the Wand of Wishing is NOT in Fort Knox, but
in the castle before entering Hell. So much the better. This remains to be
verified as a valid strategy for debug mode, much less scoring mode,
but has possibilities.

I myself don't see it as a "bug" like some old time hackers are shouting.
If I remember correctly, older versions of nethack had the amulet on level 20,
not 56 plus four more elemental planes back to the temple. With this strategy
the player still has to get down to level 26 to be able to get a wand of
wishing. 

As the number of branches in the dungeon grows, and the number of artifacts
to be gained at the end of each quest mounts, it is going to become 
unmanagable to prevent all these artifacts from being wished for. Already
there has been some disappointment by some people who wished for their
quest artifact and then confusedly still attempted to complete the quest,
but the contigency code to prevent them from doing so (if priest, can't
wish for priest quest artifact, but can wish for the knight's, the tourist's,
etc) is too large, and its really straying away from the spirit of a wish.
Instead its forcing the player to jump through a set of hoops set up by the
designers in a certain numbered order rather than actually have fun. After all,
this is a game, not an auto assembly job. The only limit should be
on wishing for more wishes.

Considering the scarcity of wishes, a better solution than the brute force
prevention of wishing for artifacts is to offer several artifacts at the bottom
of several branches that need to be found. Then wishes will become a matter
of resource management, with people maybe opting to use a wish to get a
particular quest artifact rather than complete a particular quest (maybe its 
too hard, maybe its too boring and easy now) but certainly not being able
to wish for all artifacts of all quests. Being able to pick and choose which
quests to complete is such a unix-y idea it seems natural for nethack, and
I certainly don't see why the Amulet of Yendor should be allowed to remain
above this revolution.

But of course, I really believe something should happen when you offer your
quest artifact to your god's altar (like getting an amulet of life saving
in exchange and getting your name on the scoreboard even though you're not
dead), so you can see where I'm going.


